 ## Controller
 
+### r7 bounded public publication retries
+
+`publishAdvance` now supports identical same-process retries for both pure-prefix
+and correction publications. It snapshots the input properties on the first
+call and binds the batch ID to the exact owned capture, normalized prefix count,
+next checkpoint and owned draft identity/head. Equivalent snapshots or newly
+authored equivalent drafts are not identities.
+
+* Identical calls while the send is pending share one underlying submission and
+  report the same outcome. No result is cached before the local sequenced echo;
+  even an identical peer batch is not that local echo.
+* After the local echo, an applied publication retries as `duplicate`, while
+  stale remains `stale`. This also works before Promise callbacks have run.
+* Changed capture/prefix/checkpoint/draft reuse rejects without poisoning the
+  kernel or disturbing the first publication. Changed draft heads and unfinished
+  draft transactions reject, including on retries.
+* The capture and (for corrections) draft must remain undisposed for public
+  retries. Disposing the capture expires this API retry scope but does not cancel
+  its already submitted operation. Disposing the checkout rejects pending receipts.
+* At most 128 public publication records are retained per kernel/controller.
+  Dispose completed captures to release them; disposed captures with pending sends
+  retain their records until completion. There is also a 128 in-flight core limit.
+  Capacity rejection happens before sealing a new public draft.
+
+Controllers are canonicalized by underlying kernel, not the identity of an ITree
+facade. The core submission route also checks encoded identity outside the fatal
+breaker boundary and shares an identical in-flight receipt. True submission
+failures remain fatal and reject waiting receipts with the original error;
+the breaker is never cleared. A fatally broken checkout's existing disposal API
+can itself reject, so abandon that failed instance; this is not recovery support.
+
+Released public receipts cannot be reopened or substituted with a new capture.
+Previously sequenced batch IDs still cannot be repurposed: the existing wire
+batch ledger rejects that reuse. That ledger and authority history remain
+unbounded in this prototype; the 128-record limit is **not** a total memory or
+durable deduplication guarantee. Runtime restart, expired service history,
+cross-process receipts and replay preparation remain unsupported.
+
+No API signature, envelope version, ordering service or DDS type changes in r7.
+Runtime wire duplicate/reconnect paths remain separate from public API retry.
+
+The r7 all-M4 successor also makes capture disposal reentrant-safe. It first
+preflights every reference checkout without changing ownership, then marks the
+capture expired before disposing public drafts (which can invoke caller-owned
+revertible disposal callbacks). A callback that calls `capture.dispose()` again
+is a no-op. Transaction/change-event preflight failures leave the capture usable.
+Real teardown errors are not swallowed; internal A/S checkout cleanup runs in
+`finally`, and an already-started capture disposal remains expired.
+
 ### r6 opt-in, capture-local node counterparts
 
 Ordinary `capture()` and authority wire protocol v2 are unchanged. The separate
